WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.580
Do you remember hurricanes when you were younger hitting and affecting the Ninth Ward?

2
00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.700
There was something bad in 1927, I know that.

3
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:08.900
In '47, too.

4
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.120
In 1927 they had a bad storm, yeah.

5
00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.340
I don't remember too much but it was pretty bad.

6
00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:17.440
It destroyed some houses, you know.

7
00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:19.060
But it wasn't as bad as these storms.

8
00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:22.680
See, it passed in the same way, you know.

9
00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:29.880
But in 1915, they had what they called a [?] storm and my father and them used to tell everyone the bad storms they had.

10
00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:36.080
And we never had hurricanes like that come along [at their time?] until later years, you see.

11
00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:39.540
Of course, they never worried about them too much like that.

12
00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.180
Like right now, you see,

13
00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:54.140
the first of October is hurricane season's over and you're not going to get any . . . In fact we haven't been getting any lately because every time if a hurricane's coming down and the cold front's coming down,

14
00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:59.240
the cold front would push the hurricane back, and they go up the other way.

15
00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:04.320
See, that's why we're always praying for a cold front to come in, you see.

16
00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:12.860
When I was growing up, the hurricanes was just a lot of rain, a lot of wind and maybe blew some shingles off or whatever.

17
00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:15.580
Nothing like the ones they have now.

18
00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:22.900
And then Betsy in '65, my husband and I were living in a house on [Renee?] Street,

19
00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:32.340
right on the side of the St. Claude Bridge and we got . . . It's like after the storm's over, that's when the water comes.

20
00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:38.020
Well the storm was over, everything was fine, it was morning, and then the water started coming under the walls.

21
00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:38.720
It got knee deep.

22
00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:42.300
I was talking to my mom on the phone and she says, "Oh my God, the tree's coming through the house!"

23
00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:44.160
And blomp, the phone went dead.

24
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:45.900
I didn't know if they were dead or alive.

25
00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:51.840
But the next day he came walking to get us and we walked to his car.

26
00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:55.960
But where he lived never, ever had water.

27
00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:57.960
What year did you buy the house on Douglas Street?

28
00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.720
In 1948.

29
00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:02.560
And never, ever had water.

30
00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:03.600
Katrina was the first incident . . . ?

31
00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:03.984
The first time.

32
00:02:03.984 --> 00:02:05.900
Yeah, the first time we had ever had water that year.
